   53: \tableofcontents
   54: \begin{abstract}
   55: Many flow-level network intrusion detection pipelines reduce each connection to aggregate measurements and then classify flows in isolation. This efficient abstraction suppresses packet ordering and non-uniform timing, and it can obscure evidence that becomes visible only across related connections. To retain both levels of structure, this thesis develops a hierarchical detector that does not consume payload content. Aligned packet records and flow statistics are extracted from an enterprise PCAP through a custom Suricata output module. Stage~1 combines positional and cumulative-time information when encoding the ordered packet metadata, conditions the packet tokens on flow statistics, and exports a fused representation for each flow. In Stage~2, the target representation queries a start-time-ordered history of earlier related flow embeddings before the final prediction is produced.
   56: 
   57: Chronological partitions are used throughout the evaluation. Because positive flows are scarce, the analysis gives priority to macro-F1, PR-AUC, attack-class performance, confusion matrices, and false-positive rate. On the controlled Stage~1 flow test manifest, the complete fused representation reaches a macro-F1 of 0.8954 and a PR-AUC of 0.8763, exceeding the aggregate-flow, recurrent, convolutional, and encoding controls. On the separate Stage~2 flow manifest, source-host context raises macro-F1 from 0.8954 to 0.9275 and PR-AUC from 0.8763 to 0.9345 while reducing both types of classification error. The target-query model also surpasses the no-context, vanilla Transformer, LSTM, GRU, and CNN--LSTM alternatives, with model-level latency comparable to that of the vanilla Transformer. External evaluation uses five non-overlapping 20,000-flow windows from the public CICIDS2017 Wednesday capture and a 38,513-flow company capture obtained from a different server. Pooled macro-F1 across the five public windows is 0.8966, while the separate-server capture reaches 0.9026. Taken together, the results indicate that explicit intra-flow timing and selective inter-flow context supply complementary and partly transferable evidence, although performance varies across external time windows.
   58: 
   59:   \begin{keywords}
   60:    Network intrusion detection, packet sequence, time-aware Transformer, flow representation, cross-flow context, class imbalance
   61:   \end{keywords}
   62: \end{abstract}
   63: 
   64: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   65: 
   66: \chapter{Introduction}
   67: \label{sec:introduction}
   68: 
   69: Enterprise systems, cloud platforms, mobile networks, and Internet-of-Things (IoT) deployments collectively produce large and heterogeneous traffic streams. Continuous monitoring is needed to identify activities such as reconnaissance, exploitation attempts, botnet communication, distributed denial-of-service (DDoS), and multi-stage intrusion campaigns. Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDSs) consequently form an important layer of cyber-security infrastructure \cite{Roesch1999,SommerPaxson2010}. Their practical design nevertheless remains difficult. Contemporary attacks may be distributed, adaptive, and temporally extended, while encryption restricts direct payload inspection and increases reliance on headers, metadata, and behavioural traffic characteristics \cite{Sharma2025EncryptedSurvey,Dong2025EncryptedPretrain}.
   70: 
   71: Operational deployments continue to rely on signature engines such as Snort and Suricata because their rules are interpretable, computationally efficient, and effective against known threats \cite{Roesch1999,SuricataGuide2026}. Suspicious traffic is recognised through patterns designed and maintained by domain experts. Such rules remain valuable, but their coverage is necessarily bounded: unseen attacks and evolving variants may not match an existing signature, and malicious intent may emerge only from temporal or contextual regularities spanning several flows \cite{SommerPaxson2010,Hozouri2025Survey}. Rule-based analysis alone is therefore unlikely to provide sufficient coverage as traffic volumes and attack behaviours continue to diversify.
   72: 
   73: Learning-based NIDSs seek greater adaptability by estimating decision boundaries from observed traffic rather than depending exclusively on predefined rules. In conventional pipelines, a bidirectional flow is represented by aggregate features such as its duration, packet and byte counts, rates, and inter-arrival-time statistics \cite{BuczakGuven2016,Ahmed2016Survey}. Compact fixed-length vectors support efficient large-scale processing, but the compression removes detail about the process that generated each flow. Packet order, bursts, directional transitions, and uneven temporal gaps can all disappear during aggregation. For attacks whose distinguishing evidence lies in how a connection unfolds rather than only in its total volume, that information loss is consequential.
   74: 
   75: Richer traffic representations have been investigated with convolutional and recurrent neural networks, LSTMs, GRUs, and autoencoder-based architectures \cite{Yin2017RNNIDS,Shone2018NNAE,Mirsky2018Kitsune}. These models can capture non-linear feature interactions while reducing manual feature engineering. Yet their inputs often remain aggregate flow records. In some sequence-based studies, the sequence is formed from fixed-size flow attributes rather than the packets observed within a connection. Such formulations preserve only part of the natural hierarchy of network traffic: packets constitute flows, and related flows in turn express host- or endpoint-level behaviour.
   76: 
   77: Self-attention offers a suitable mechanism for addressing this hierarchy because distant sequence elements can interact directly and training can be parallelised \cite{Vaswani2017}. Transformer-based systems have consequently achieved competitive results in traffic classification and intrusion detection \cite{Wu2022RTIDS,Han2023GTID,Manocchio2024FlowTransformer}. Time-aware variants show, in particular, that detection can benefit when order and elapsed time are encoded as distinct signals \cite{Han2023GTID}; FlowTransformer demonstrates the architectural flexibility available for flow-based NIDS evaluation \cite{Manocchio2024FlowTransformer}. Even so, a substantial portion of this literature continues to represent each flow as one flat item. Packet-level dynamics inside a flow and contextual dependencies between flows are seldom modelled together.
   78: 
   79: To address this gap, the thesis organises detection into two stages. Raw PCAP traffic is first converted into aligned packet records and flow statistics by a custom Suricata output module. Stage~1 derives a flow representation from packet order, elapsed time, and aggregate measurements. A bounded causal history of related representations is then used by Stage~2 to refine the flow-level decision. Separating these operations permits intra-flow representation learning and inter-flow reasoning to be assessed independently without changing the final prediction granularity.
   80: 
   81: The central research problem of this thesis is therefore:
   82: 
   83: \begin{displayquote}
   84: How can network intrusion detection benefit from packet-level temporal modelling and cross-flow contextual modelling while preserving the hierarchical structure of network traffic?
   85: \end{displayquote}
   86: 
   87: To answer this question, the thesis studies two research questions:
   88: 
   89: \begin{itemize}
   90:     \item \textbf{RQ1:} Does time-aware packet-sequence modelling produce more informative flow representations than flow-level aggregation alone?
   91:     \item \textbf{RQ2:} Does incorporating cross-flow context improve flow-level intrusion detection compared with independent per-flow classification?
   92: \end{itemize}
   93: 
   94: The thesis makes three principal contributions:
   95: 
   96: \begin{itemize}
   97:     \item An aligned PCAP-to-learning pipeline built around a custom Suricata output module, with explicit separation among predictive variables, context identifiers, and alert-derived targets.
   98:     \item A Stage~1 representation that integrates packet position, cumulative elapsed time, mask-aware self-attention, and bounded flow-conditioned token modulation.
   99:     \item A causal Stage~2 target-query attention model that draws evidence from relation-specific histories while excluding future flows and historical labels.
  100:     \item External evaluation on chronological windows from the public CICIDS2017 Wednesday capture and on a separate company-provided server capture, complementing the source-domain chronological test.
  101:     % \item A controlled evaluation of representation, fusion, sequence length, context relation, context capacity, architecture, and computational cost under class imbalance.
  102: \end{itemize}
  103: 
  104: Given the low prevalence of the positive class, macro-F1, PR-AUC, attack-class performance, and false-positive rate carry more interpretive weight than accuracy alone.
  105: 
  106: \chapter{Related Work}
  107: \label{sec:related_work}
  108: 
  109: Research on network intrusion detection spans manually specified signatures and data-driven models learned from packet traces, flow records, or ordered network events. The literature can be organised along two dimensions: representation granularity and contextual scope. Inputs range from packet bytes and header sequences to aggregate flow statistics and learned embeddings. Most systems assign labels independently to individual packets or flows; comparatively fewer studies represent temporal or relational dependencies across several flows.
  110: 
  111: \section{Signature-Based and Statistical Flow-Based Detection}
  112: \label{subsec:rw_signature_flow}
  113: 
  114: Expert-authored rules are the basis of signature-driven NIDSs. Snort introduced a lightweight, extensible design for real-time packet inspection \cite{Roesch1999}; Suricata extends this operational model with multi-threaded processing and programmable logging \cite{SuricataGuide2026}. Their alerts are interpretable and rule behaviour can be controlled, which preserves their operational relevance. Coverage, however, depends on maintaining an appropriate signature set, and new or behaviourally altered attacks may fall outside it. Sommer and Paxson place this limitation in a broader methodological context: learning-based NIDS research must account for both the open-world nature of traffic and the semantic distance between laboratory benchmarks and deployed networks \cite{SommerPaxson2010}.
  115: 
  116: Classical machine-learning pipelines usually group packets into bidirectional flows and describe each flow through duration, volume, rate, TCP-flag, and inter-arrival summaries. Ahmed et al. and Buczak and Guven review the use of decision trees, support-vector machines, nearest-neighbour methods, clustering, and ensembles for anomaly and intrusion detection \cite{Ahmed2016Survey,BuczakGuven2016}; conventional models can also benefit from feature selection and hybrid classification \cite{Aljawarneh2018}. Aggregation converts a variable-length conversation into an efficient fixed-dimensional vector. That convenience entails a loss of process information: similar totals may arise from different packet orders, directional changes, burst structures, or temporal gaps. A model restricted to aggregate statistics cannot observe those generating dynamics directly.
  117: 
  118: \section{Deep Learning for Packet and Flow Sequences}
  119: \label{subsec:rw_deep_sequences}
  120: 
  121: Deep architectures learn non-linear traffic representations and thereby reduce reliance on manually selected feature combinations. Recurrent NIDSs maintain a hidden state over ordered observations; Yin et al., for example, demonstrated that recurrent neural networks can capture sequential dependencies in intrusion data \cite{Yin2017RNNIDS}. Shone et al. paired a non-symmetric deep autoencoder with a supervised classifier to derive compact representations \cite{Shone2018NNAE}. Kitsune pursued lightweight online anomaly detection through an ensemble of incrementally processed autoencoders \cite{Mirsky2018Kitsune}. Local pattern extraction and recurrent temporal modelling have also been combined in CNN--LSTM hybrids \cite{Ullah2024IDSINT}.
  122: 
  123: Architecture alone does not determine whether temporal structure is learned; the construction of the sequence is equally important. An LSTM or GRU is sometimes applied across the dimensions of one flow vector, so the resulting ``sequence'' represents feature order rather than chronological packets or flows. Feature interactions may be learned in this setting, but network time is not necessarily represented. NetSentry adopts a more direct temporal formulation by processing ordered observations for early detection of large-scale attacks \cite{LiuPatras2022NetSentry}. Its findings support the value of temporal correlation beyond independent record classification. Recurrent computation nevertheless proceeds sequentially, and compressing a long or irregularly sampled history into one final state can create an information bottleneck.
  124: 
  125: \section{Transformer-Based Traffic Representation}
  126: \label{subsec:rw_transformers}
  127: 
  128: By replacing recurrent transitions with self-attention, Transformers permit direct interaction between distant tokens and support parallel training \cite{Vaswani2017}. Both properties are relevant when evidence is separated by numerous packets or flows. RTIDS applied this mechanism to robust intrusion classification on flow-oriented benchmarks \cite{Wu2022RTIDS}. An encoder-based Transformer was later examined for cloud network intrusion detection by Long et al. \cite{long2024transformer}, whereas IDS-INT combined Transformer transfer learning, imbalance handling, and downstream neural components \cite{Ullah2024IDSINT}.
  129: 
  130: GTID combines packet- and session-level features in a time-aware Transformer whose temporal coordinate is derived from inter-packet intervals rather than ordinal position alone \cite{Han2023GTID}. FlowTransformer takes a complementary, modular approach, enabling input encodings, Transformer blocks, classification heads, parameter counts, and runtime to be examined on flow-based NIDS datasets \cite{Manocchio2024FlowTransformer}. The memory flow Transformer extends attention to longer flow histories and complex relations in large sequential inputs \cite{Jiang2025MFT}. Together, these studies show that dependencies lost in a single aggregate vector can be recovered by attention-based models. They also make clear that encoding, pooling, sequence definition, and computational cost are at least as consequential as the choice of model family.
  131: 
  132: Three unresolved distinctions motivate the present hierarchy. Ordinal adjacency does not reveal whether two packets are separated by microseconds or seconds. Packet sequences and aggregate statistics also describe different properties of the same connection. Finally, even a rich representation of one flow cannot establish on its own whether that flow belongs to a distributed host-level pattern. The proposed design treats these as separate representational requirements.
  133: 
  134: \section{Self-Supervised and Encrypted-Traffic Representation Learning}
  135: \label{subsec:rw_ssl_encrypted}
  136: 
  137: As encryption restricts payload inspection, traffic analysis has increasingly turned to packet sizes, directions, timing, header metadata, and learned latent representations. Surveys of encrypted-traffic analysis document this change and the expanding role of deep pre-training \cite{Sharma2025EncryptedSurvey,Dong2025EncryptedPretrain}. ET-BERT transfers bidirectional Transformer pre-training to contextualised datagram representations for encrypted-traffic classification \cite{Lin2022ETBERT}. Masked autoencoders learn from unlabelled records by reconstructing concealed parts of an input \cite{Zhao2024MAETraffic,2024MAETraffic}, while contrastive objectives improve label efficiency by aligning compatible traffic views and separating incompatible ones \cite{Li2024Contrastive}.
  138: 
  139: Recent work has also widened the range of admissible representations. CoTNeT applies contextual Transformer processing to encrypted-traffic features \cite{Huang2024CoTNet}; DoLLM reformulates flow records for large-language-model-based DDoS detection \cite{Li2024DoLLM}. Both illustrate that plaintext payloads are not a prerequisite for useful representation learning. Nevertheless, encrypted-traffic classification should not be conflated with intrusion detection. An application's identity can remain stable even when malicious behaviour is sparse, evolving, or distributed over several connections.
  140: 
  141: \section{Temporal, Relational, and Cross-Flow Context}
  142: \label{subsec:rw_cross_flow}
  143: 
  144: Conditional independence between flows is a convenient assumption for batching and online inference, but many attack processes violate it. Scanning, password guessing, command and control, DDoS, and lateral movement may each generate a collection of individually ambiguous flows whose shared source, destination, endpoint, or temporal pattern is informative. NetSentry demonstrates the value of preserving observation order \cite{LiuPatras2022NetSentry}. Graph-based NIDSs instead encode host and flow relations explicitly: Deng et al. use graph convolution over flow topology for label-limited IoT detection \cite{Deng2023FlowTopology}, and Xu et al. combine graph neural networks with self-supervised learning for network-flow intrusion detection \cite{Xu2024SSLGNN}. Zhong et al. survey this expanding area and emphasise the capacity of graph models to represent structural dependence \cite{Zhong2024GNNSurvey}.
  145: 
  146: Relational inductive bias is expressive, although maintaining a large communication graph can increase memory demand, latency, and deployment complexity. Window-based sequences occupy a different point in this design space. One recent Transformer groups IoT flows into temporal windows and represents every flow as a token, allo…28117 tokens truncated…ver the highest-recall recurrent baseline is substantial.
 1633: 
 1634: This contrast is consistent with the intended inductive bias. Vanilla self-attention updates the full context sequence and classifies its final valid state; the proposed encoder keeps the current flow as the query and gates the retrieved historical summary. Simultaneous improvement in macro-F1 and PR-AUC is compatible with stronger target-centred ranking of positive examples. It is not, however, a clean isolation of the query pattern, because other architectural choices and parameter counts also differ.
 1635: 
 1636: With 514 parameters, the no-context head is far smaller than the 283,522-parameter Stage~2 model. It should be read as a current-flow-only lower bound, not a capacity-matched ablation. Its result shows that the minimal independent head is insufficient, but does not identify how much of the full gap comes from history. A stricter control would preserve the target-query architecture and comparable capacity while masking every historical item.
 1637: 
 1638: \subsection{Efficiency and Operational Trade-Offs}
 1639: \label{subsec:results_statistical_efficiency}
 1640: 
 1641: Parameter count and measured evaluation latency are related in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage2_efficiency}, with macro-F1 annotated for each architecture. A logarithmic parameter axis accommodates the range from 514 to more than 1.2 million parameters.
