  642: 
  643: \begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{L\arabic*:},leftmargin=*]
  644:     \item \textbf{Alert-derived ground truth.} Label coverage and error reflect a single Suricata ruleset. A negative record is therefore more accurately interpreted as ``no alert observed'' than as independently verified benign traffic.
  645: 
  646:     \item \textbf{Limited multi-source scope.} Model development and ablation evidence comes from one company source capture. The public CICIDS2017 windows and separate-server company capture extend evaluation beyond that source, but two external sources cannot establish generalisation across arbitrary organisations, sensors, rulesets, or extended periods.
  647: 
  648:     \item \textbf{Capture visibility.} Only traffic observable at the collection point appears in the PCAP. Packet loss, asymmetric routing, encryption, and incomplete flows may change the extracted measurements. Where organisational policy allows, documentation should include the collection tool, interface location, capture interval, packet-loss report, and snap length.
  649: 
  650:     \item \textbf{Sensitive identifiers.} Stage~2 relations are currently formed from addresses retained in local metadata. Those addresses must be excluded from the thesis, shared CSVs, screenshots, and public metadata. Any released artefact requires stable pseudonymisation verified to preserve equality-based context membership.
  651: \end{enumerate}
  652: 
  653: Within these limits, the study evaluates a traceable learning pipeline on a large proprietary capture with weak labels. Suricata outputs are not treated as perfect attack truth, the presence of every zero-day behaviour is not assumed, and no claim is made that performance transfers unchanged to other enterprise networks. Tensors, masks, flow measurements, and causal-context metadata together define the interface passed between the two model stages.
