  656: 
  657: \chapter{Proposed Methodology}

=== 658-997 ===
  658: \label{sec:proposed_methodology}
  659: 
  660: Two complementary scales of traffic structure are represented by the detector. Stage~1 operates within a bidirectional flow, combining its ordered packets with statistical properties. Stage~2 relates the target flow to eligible historical flows. Sequential training and a fixed-dimensional Stage~1 interface keep intra-flow representation learning distinct from contextual reasoning across flows.
  661: 
  662: Although the architecture is derived from the Transformer \cite{Vaswani2017}, self-attention alone does not define the method. Three mechanisms are combined: (i) a cumulative-time coordinate that represents irregular packet arrival, (ii) an independently encoded statistical branch that modulates packet tokens within a bounded range, and (iii) causal target-query attention from the current flow to historical flows only. The distinction between packet order and elapsed time follows evidence from time-aware NIDS research \cite{Han2023GTID}. Separating within-flow evidence from cross-flow behaviour also addresses attacks whose signal is dispersed over several connections and may be missed when flows are classified independently
  663: \cite{LiuPatras2022NetSentry,Manocchio2024FlowTransformer, MartinReizabal2026FlowWindow}.
  664: 
  665: The high-level data path appears in Figure~\ref{fig:overall_framework}. A time-aware intra-flow Transformer converts packet records into one embedding for each flow. A sequence of these embeddings is then contextualised in Stage~2 to produce a binary decision for the target flow. Figure~\ref{fig:method_architecture} gives a more detailed view of statistical conditioning and target-query attention.
  666: 
  667: \begin{figure}[H]
  668:     \centering
  669:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{MScCS/overallFramework.png}
  670:     \caption{High-level architecture of the hierarchical detector. Stage~1 transforms a packet sequence into an intra-flow representation, and Stage~2 contextualises the resulting flow sequence before classifying the target flow.}
  671:     \label{fig:overall_framework}
  672: \end{figure}
  673: 
  674: \section{Architectural Overview}
  675: \label{subsec:method_architecture}
  676: 
  677: Flow $i$ is represented by a selected and padded packet-feature matrix
  678: $\mathbf{X}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d_p}$, a packet inter-arrival tensor
  679: $\mathbf{u}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$, a validity mask
  680: $\mathbf{m}_i\in\{0,1\}^{L}$, a statistical flow vector
  681: $\mathbf{s}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{d_f}$, and a binary target
  682: $y_i\in\{0,1\}$. The principal configuration uses $L=128$. Stage~1 computes
  683: 
  684: \begin{equation}
  685:     \left(\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(1)}_i,\mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i\right)
  686:     =F_{\theta_1}
  687:     \left(
  688:         \mathbf{X}_i,\mathbf{u}_i,\mathbf{m}_i,\mathbf{s}_i
  689:     \right),
  690:     \label{eq:method_stage1_mapping}
  691: \end{equation}
  692: 
  693: Here, $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(1)}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$ denotes the Stage~1 logits, and $\mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ is the fused representation supplied to Stage~2. The principal model sets $d=128$.
  694: 
  695: Once all Stage~1 embeddings are available, flows are stably ordered by start time and flow identifier. Given relation $r$ and target $i$, the bounded causal input to Stage~2 is
  696: 
  697: \begin{equation}
  698:     \mathbf{Z}^{(r)}_i=
  699:     \left[
  700:         \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_{j_1},\ldots,
  701:         \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_{j_{K_i}},
  702:         \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i
  703:     \right],
  704:     \qquad j_k<i,\quad K_i\leq W-1,
  705:     \label{eq:method_stage2_input}
  706: \end{equation}
  707: 
  708: The current flow occupies the final valid position, and the principal setting uses $W=128$ tokens including that target. Stage~2 computes
  709: 
  710: \begin{equation}
  711:     \boldsymbol{\ell}^{(2,r)}_i
  712:     =G_{\theta_2}
  713:     \left(\mathbf{Z}^{(r)}_i,\boldsymbol{\mu}_i\right),
  714:     \label{eq:method_stage2_mapping}
  715: \end{equation}
  716: 
  717: where left padding is excluded by $\boldsymbol{\mu}_i$. Applying softmax to $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(2,r)}_i$ yields the final class-1 probability.
  718: 
  719: As indicated in Figure~\ref{fig:method_architecture}, labels and Suricata alert fields serve only as targets and are absent from both encoders.
  720: 
  721: \begin{figure}[H]
  722:     \centering
  723:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{MScCS/ArchitecturalOverview.png}
  724:     \caption{Detailed architecture of the proposed two-stage detector. The statistical branch conditions Stage~1 packet tokens, while Stage~2 uses the target flow as the query and historical flows as keys and values.}
  725:     \label{fig:method_architecture}
  726: \end{figure}
  727: 
  728: \section{Stage~1: Intra-Flow Time-Aware Representation}
  729: \label{subsec:method_stage1}
  730: 
  731: A variable-length packet sequence is reduced by Stage~1 to one flow embedding. The principal processing path comprises record projection, time-aware sinusoidal encoding, flow-conditioned token modulation, pre-normalised Transformer layers, hybrid mask-aware pooling, and a two-class head.
  732: 
  733: \subsection{Packet-Record Projection}
  734: \label{subsubsec:packet_projection}
  735: 
  736: At packet position $t$, the preprocessed vector $\mathbf{x}_{i,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_p}$ is mapped into the model space:
  737: 
  738: \begin{equation}
  739:     \mathbf{e}_{i,t}
  740:     =\operatorname{LN}
  741:     \left(W_p\mathbf{x}_{i,t}+\mathbf{b}_p\right),
  742:     \qquad
  743:     \mathbf{e}_{i,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}.
  744:     \label{eq:record_projection}
  745: \end{equation}
  746: 
  747: An MLP projection is supported, although the principal configuration applies a single linear map followed by layer normalisation. As specified in Chapter~\ref{sec:dataset_preprocessing}, categorical fields have already been one-hot encoded, numerical variables transformed and robustly scaled, and binary indicators retained in $\{0,1\}$.
  748: 
  749: \subsection{Cumulative Time-Aware Encoding}
  750: \label{subsubsec:time_aware_encoding}
  751: 
  752: Sinusoidal positional encoding captures ordinal packet order but assigns the same adjacency to gaps of microseconds and seconds. Stage~1 augments position with cumulative elapsed time. Preprocessing stores
  753: 
  754: \begin{equation}
  755:     u_{i,t}=\log\left(1+\Delta\tau_{i,t}\right),
  756:     \label{eq:stored_log_iat}
  757: \end{equation}
  758: 
  759: where $\Delta\tau_{i,t}$ is the nonnegative microsecond gap from the preceding packet in the same bidirectional flow. The model reconstructs and masks this interval as
  760: 
  761: \begin{equation}
  762:     \widehat{\Delta\tau}_{i,t}
  763:     =m_{i,t}\max\left(0,\exp(u_{i,t})-1\right).
  764:     \label{eq:reconstructed_iat}
  765: \end{equation}
  766: 
  767: Cumulative time and its smoothed logarithmic form are then
  768: 
  769: \begin{align}
  770:     c_{i,t} &= \sum_{k=1}^{t}\widehat{\Delta\tau}_{i,k},\\
  771:     p_{i,t} &= \log\left(1+c_{i,t}+\alpha\right),
  772:     \qquad \alpha=10^{-7}.
  773:     \label{eq:cumulative_time_feature}
  774: \end{align}
  775: 
  776: With zero-based position $q_t=t-1$, order and time define the coordinate $r_{i,t}=q_t+p_{i,t}$. Channel pair $j$ is encoded by
  777: 
  778: \begin{align}
  779:     \operatorname{TE}_{i,t,2j}
  780:     &=\sin\left(
  781:         \frac{r_{i,t}}{10000^{2j/d}}
  782:       \right),\\
  783:     \operatorname{TE}_{i,t,2j+1}
  784:     &=\cos\left(
  785:         \frac{r_{i,t}}{10000^{2j/d}}
  786:       \right).
  787:     \label{eq:time_aware_sinusoid}
  788: \end{align}
  789: 
  790: Padding positions are assigned zero encodings. A valid packet token becomes
  791: 
  792: \begin{equation}
  793:     \widetilde{\mathbf{e}}_{i,t}
  794:     =\operatorname{Dropout}
  795:     \left(
  796:         \mathbf{e}_{i,t}+m_{i,t}\operatorname{TE}_{i,t}
  797:     \right).
  798:     \label{eq:time_encoded_packet}
  799: \end{equation}
  800: 
  801: This formulation adapts the time-aware Transformer principle in GTID \cite{Han2023GTID} to structured packet metadata without payload content. Two ablations isolate its coordinates: time-only encoding sets $r_{i,t}=p_{i,t}$, whereas position-only encoding sets $r_{i,t}=q_t$.
  802: 
  803: \subsection{Flow-Statistics Encoder}
  804: \label{subsubsec:flow_encoder}
  805: 
  806: A bottleneck encoder maps the separately preprocessed flow vector $\mathbf{s}_i$ to the packet-model dimension:
  807: 
  808: \begin{equation}
  809:     \mathbf{f}_i
  810:     =\operatorname{LN}_2
  811:     \left(
  812:         W_{f,2}\,
  813:         \operatorname{Dropout}
  814:         \left(
  815:             \operatorname{GELU}
  816:             \left(
  817:                 \operatorname{LN}_1(W_{f,1}\mathbf{s}_i+\mathbf{b}_{f,1})
  818:             \right)
  819:         \right)
  820:         +\mathbf{b}_{f,2}
  821:     \right).
  822:     \label{eq:flow_feature_encoder}
  823: \end{equation}
  824: 
  825: Its principal hidden and output dimensions are 32 and 128, respectively. Restricting the statistical branch in this way reduces the chance that a high-dimensional aggregate vector dominates evidence learned from packets.
  826: 
  827: \subsection{Bounded Flow-Conditioned Token Modulation}
  828: \label{subsubsec:token_film}
  829: 
  830: Feature-wise linear modulation (FiLM) motivates the principal Stage~1 fusion mechanism: conditioning information generates affine parameters for individual features \cite{Perez2018FiLM}. A small network derives from the flow representation
  831: 
  832: \begin{equation}
  833:     [\boldsymbol{\gamma}_i;\boldsymbol{\beta}_i]
  834:     =G_f(\mathbf{f}_i),
  835:     \qquad
  836:     \boldsymbol{\gamma}_i,\boldsymbol{\beta}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{d}.
  837:     \label{eq:film_parameters}
  838: \end{equation}
  839: 
  840: The unbounded flow-conditioned residual for packet token $t$ is
  841: 
  842: \begin{equation}
  843:     \mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{raw}}_{i,t}
  844:     =\eta
  845:     \left(
  846:         \boldsymbol{\gamma}_i\odot\widetilde{\mathbf{e}}_{i,t}
  847:         +\boldsymbol{\beta}_i
  848:     \right),
  849:     \qquad
  850:     \eta=\sigma(a),\qquad a_{0}=-5,
  851:     \label{eq:film_raw_residual}
  852: \end{equation}
  853: 
  854: where $\odot$ is element-wise multiplication and the initial scale is $\sigma(-5)\approx0.0067$. A second constraint bounds this residual relative to the packet-token norm. For the configured maximum ratio $\rho=0.13$,
  855: 
  856: \begin{align}
  857:     \lambda_{i,t}
  858:     &=\min\left(
  859:         1,
  860:         \frac{\rho\|\widetilde{\mathbf{e}}_{i,t}\|_2}
  861:              {\|\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{raw}}_{i,t}\|_2+\epsilon}
  862:       \right),\\
  863:     \mathbf{e}^{F}_{i,t}
  864:     &=\widetilde{\mathbf{e}}_{i,t}
  865:       +\lambda_{i,t}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{raw}}_{i,t}.
  866:     \label{eq:bounded_token_film}
  867: \end{align}
  868: 
  869: Zero initialisation is used for the layer that produces $\boldsymbol{\gamma}_i$ and $\boldsymbol{\beta}_i$. Training therefore starts from the packet-only pathway, after which a bounded statistical correction can be learned. Such a constraint is useful when aggregate features are informative yet potentially noisy or capture-specific.
  870: 
  871: Because the statistical vector is encoded separately and then modulates packet tokens before self-attention, the principal configuration constitutes \emph{separate-branch conditional intermediate fusion}, rather than strict late fusion after pooling. Although a post-pooling gated alternative is implemented, the reported Scheme~C results use the bounded token-FiLM operation in Equation~\ref{eq:bounded_token_film}.
  872: 
  873: \subsection{Masked Transformer Encoder}
  874: \label{subsubsec:stage1_transformer}
  875: 
  876: Pre-normalised Transformer layers process the conditioned sequence $\mathbf{E}^{F}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}$. At layer $\ell$,
  877: 
  878: \begin{align}
  879:     \mathbf{A}^{\ell}_i
  880:     &=\operatorname{MHA}
  881:       \left(
  882:         \operatorname{LN}(\mathbf{H}^{\ell-1}_i),
  883:         \operatorname{LN}(\mathbf{H}^{\ell-1}_i),
  884:         \operatorname{LN}(\mathbf{H}^{\ell-1}_i);
  885:         \mathbf{m}_i
  886:       \right),\\
  887:     \overline{\mathbf{H}}^{\ell}_i
  888:     &=\mathbf{H}^{\ell-1}_i
  889:       +\operatorname{Dropout}(\mathbf{A}^{\ell}_i),\\
  890:     \mathbf{H}^{\ell}_i
  891:     &=\overline{\mathbf{H}}^{\ell}_i
  892:       +\operatorname{FFN}
  893:        \left(\operatorname{LN}(\overline{\mathbf{H}}^{\ell}_i)\right),
  894:     \label{eq:stage1_transformer_layer}
  895: \end{align}
  896: 
  897: with $\mathbf{H}^{0}_i=\mathbf{E}^{F}_i$. Scaled dot-product attention is applied in every head:
  898: 
  899: \begin{equation}
  900:     \operatorname{Attn}(Q,K,V)
  901:     =\operatorname{softmax}
  902:       \left(
  903:         \frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_h}}+M_i
  904:       \right)V,
  905:     \label{eq:scaled_dot_product_attention}
  906: \end{equation}
  907: 
  908: Here, $M_i$ assigns negative infinity to padded keys. GELU is used in the feed-forward sublayer. The principal encoder contains two layers and four attention heads, with model dimension 128, feed-forward dimension 256, and dropout 0.15.
  909: 
  910: Stage~1 does not apply a causal mask, so every retained packet in the completed or truncated flow may attend to every other retained packet. The stage should therefore be interpreted as flow-level classification, not packet-by-packet early detection. Padding is excluded both as an attention key and during pooling.
  911: 
  912: \subsection{Hybrid Mask-Aware Pooling}
  913: \label{subsubsec:hybrid_pooling}
  914: 
  915: Three complementary summaries reduce the packet states. First, each valid token receives a learned attention score:
  916: 
  917: \begin{align}
  918:     a_{i,t}
  919:     &=\mathbf{w}_{a,2}^{\top}
  920:       \operatorname{GELU}
  921:       \left(W_{a,1}\mathbf{h}_{i,t}+\mathbf{b}_{a,1}\right)+b_{a,2},\\
  922:     \omega_{i,t}
  923:     &=\frac{m_{i,t}\exp(a_{i,t})}
  924:       {\sum_{k=1}^{L}m_{i,k}\exp(a_{i,k})},\\
  925:     \mathbf{z}^{\mathrm{att}}_i
  926:     &=\sum_{t=1}^{L}\omega_{i,t}\mathbf{h}_{i,t}.
  927:     \label{eq:stage1_attention_pooling}
  928: \end{align}
  929: 
  930: Mask-aware mean and maximum summaries are
  931: 
  932: \begin{align}
  933:     \mathbf{z}^{\mathrm{mean}}_i
  934:     &=\frac{\sum_{t=1}^{L}m_{i,t}\mathbf{h}_{i,t}}
  935:             {\max(1,\sum_{t=1}^{L}m_{i,t})},\\
  936:     \mathbf{z}^{\mathrm{max}}_i
  937:     &=\max_{t:m_{i,t}=1}\mathbf{h}_{i,t}.
  938:     \label{eq:mean_max_pooling}
  939: \end{align}
  940: 
  941: All three summaries contribute to the Stage~1 embedding:
  942: 
  943: \begin{equation}
  944:     \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i
  945:     =\operatorname{Dropout}
  946:       \left(
  947:         \operatorname{GELU}
  948:         \left(
  949:           \operatorname{LN}
  950:           \left(
  951:             W_o
  952:             [\mathbf{z}^{\mathrm{att}}_i;
  953:              \mathbf{z}^{\mathrm{mean}}_i;
  954:              \mathbf{z}^{\mathrm{max}}_i]
  955:             +\mathbf{b}_o
  956:           \right)
  957:         \right)
  958:       \right).
  959:     \label{eq:hybrid_pooling}
  960: \end{equation}
  961: 
  962: Attention pooling can concentrate on a few informative packets, the mean captures overall behaviour, and the maximum retains strong local activations. Projecting their concatenation back to dimension $d$ gives Stage~2 a fixed-size vector for every flow.
  963: 
  964: Stage~1 classification logits are
  965: 
  966: \begin{equation}
  967:     \boldsymbol{\ell}^{(1)}_i
  968:     =h_1(\mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i),
  969:     \qquad
  970:     \mathbf{p}^{(1)}_i
  971:     =\operatorname{softmax}(\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(1)}_i).
  972:     \label{eq:stage1_classifier}
  973: \end{equation}
  974: 
  975: Export occurs before the final two-class layer, preserving a multi-dimensional representation for contextual refinement instead of reducing the flow to one probability.
  976: 
  977: \subsection{Stage~1 Fusion Variants}
  978: \label{subsubsec:stage1_variants}
  979: 
  980: Table~\ref{tab:stage1_fusion_variants} defines three controlled variants. Unless stated otherwise, packet features, sequence policy, data split, Transformer capacity, optimisation procedure, and classifier are held constant across them.
  981: 
  982: \begin{table}[H]
  983:     \centering
  984:     \caption{Stage~1 packet/flow integration variants.}
  985:     \label{tab:stage1_fusion_variants}
  986:     \small
  987:     \begin{tabular}{@{}p{1.1cm}p{3.1cm}p{8.9cm}@{}}
