  998: \section{Stage~2: Causal Inter-Flow Context Modelling}
  999: \label{subsec:method_stage2}
 1000: 
 1001: Stage~2 tests whether recent related behaviour changes the interpretation of a target flow. Its inputs are restricted to Stage~1 embeddings and context metadata; raw packet fields, aggregate flow features, and historical labels are not loaded.
 1002: 
 1003: \subsection{Embedding Interface and Chronological Order}
 1004: \label{subsubsec:embedding_interface}
 1005: 
 1006: For each split, Stage~1 exports
 1007: 
 1008: \begin{equation}
 1009:     \left(
 1010:       \operatorname{flow\_id}_i,
 1011:       y_i,
 1012:       \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i,
 1013:       t_i^{0},u_i,v_i,\rho_i
 1014:     \right),
 1015:     \label{eq:stage1_export_tuple}
 1016: \end{equation}
 1017: 
 1018: where $t_i^{0}$ gives the start time, $u_i$ and $v_i$ identify source and destination, and $\rho_i$ records split membership. Stage~2 verifies identifier uniqueness, concatenates the split files, and applies a stable sort on $(t_i^{0},\operatorname{flow\_id}_i)$. Embeddings and metadata are reordered together to preserve alignment.
 1019: 
 1020: A deterministic loader without sampling is used for embedding export. The weighted training sampler draws with replacement and would otherwise repeat some flows while omitting others; it is therefore restricted to optimisation batches.
 1021: 
 1022: \subsection{Relation-Specific Causal Contexts}
 1023: \label{subsubsec:context_relations}
 1024: 
 1025: Context construction proceeds once through the sorted flows in temporal order. Eligible earlier indices are retrieved before target $i$ is added to any history. Table~\ref{tab:context_relations} defines the four relations considered.
 1026: 
 1027: \begin{table}[H]
 1028:     \centering
 1029:     \caption{Stage~2 context relations.}
 1030:     \label{tab:context_relations}
 1031:     \small
 1032:     \begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.0cm}p{4.0cm}p{7.1cm}@{}}
 1033:         \toprule
 1034:         Method & Eligibility condition for $j<i$ & Interpretation \\
 1035:         \midrule
 1036:         \methodfield{time_only} & Always eligible & Most recent flows globally, regardless of endpoint identity. \\
 1037:         \methodfield{source_host} & $u_j=u_i$ & Earlier flows initiated by the same source identifier. \\
 1038:         \methodfield{destination_host} & $v_j=v_i$ & Earlier flows directed to the same destination identifier. \\
 1039:         \methodfield{endpoint} &
 1040:         $\{u_j,v_j\}\cap\{u_i,v_i\}\neq\varnothing$ & Earlier flows sharing at least one endpoint in either role. \\
 1041:         \bottomrule
 1042:     \end{tabular}
 1043: \end{table}
 1044: 
 1045: For a given relation $r$, define
 1046: 
 1047: \begin{equation}
 1048:     \mathcal{A}^{(r)}_i
 1049:     =\left\{j<i:R_r(i,j)=1\right\}.
 1050:     \label{eq:method_eligible_context}
 1051: \end{equation}
 1052: 
 1053: Split-policy filtering and deduplication are applied before retaining the latest $W-1$ indices:
 1054: 
 1055: \begin{equation}
 1056:     \mathcal{C}^{(r)}_i
 1057:     =\operatorname{Recent}_{W-1}
 1058:       \left(\mathcal{A}^{(r)}_i\right).
 1059:     \label{eq:recent_context}
 1060: \end{equation}
 1061: 
 1062: Appending the current flow yields Equation~\ref{eq:method_stage2_input}. Histories are not updated until this sequence has been formed, preventing both self-inclusion and access to future items. Under the principal \methodfield{online} policy, a validation target may retrieve earlier training flows; a test target may retrieve earlier training, validation, and test flows. Future flows and historical labels remain unavailable. This policy approximates a detector whose state persists through chronological deployment.
 1063: 
 1064: In this implementation, ``causal'' refers to stable flow-start order: every context index occurs before its target index. The stronger condition $t_j^{\mathrm{end}}\leq t_i^{0}$ is not enforced during Stage~2 export. A long-lived overlapping flow can therefore precede the target even when its complete embedding would not have been available at the target's start time. What is evaluated is thus start-ordered, label-free context, rather than a fully timed streaming system. Production eligibility should instead be based on flow completion or embedding availability.
 1065: 
 1066: The \methodfield{time_only} relation denotes global chronological recency; it does not provide continuous inter-flow gaps to Stage~2. Explicit microsecond timing is confined to Stage~1, whereas Stage~2 represents relative age within the selected context.
 1067: 
 1068: Short sequences are padded on the left during collation, keeping the target at the final valid position:
 1069: 
 1070: \begin{equation}
 1071:     [\mathrm{PAD},\ldots,\mathrm{PAD},
 1072:       \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_{j_1},\ldots,
 1073:       \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_{j_{K_i}},
 1074:       \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i].
 1075:     \label{eq:left_padded_context}
 1076: \end{equation}
 1077: 
 1078: A Boolean mask prevents the physical padding positions from affecting either positional encoding or attention.
 1079: 
 1080: \subsection{Context-Age Positional Encoding}
 1081: \label{subsubsec:context_age_encoding}
 1082: 
 1083: For a target with $K_i$ historical tokens, age 0 is assigned to the target, age 1 to the most recent historical flow, and age $K_i$ to the oldest retained item. Writing this index as $a_{i,k}$, Stage~2 adds the sinusoidal encoding
 1084: 
 1085: \begin{align}
 1086:     \operatorname{PE}_{i,k,2j}
 1087:     &=\sin\left(
 1088:         \frac{a_{i,k}}{10000^{2j/d}}
 1089:       \right),\\
 1090:     \operatorname{PE}_{i,k,2j+1}
 1091:     &=\cos\left(
 1092:         \frac{a_{i,k}}{10000^{2j/d}}
 1093:       \right).
 1094:     \label{eq:stage2_age_encoding}
 1095: \end{align}
 1096: 
 1097: Because age is independent of absolute tensor position, additional left padding does not change it. Identical target-history orderings consequently receive identical encodings even when batch sequence lengths differ.
 1098: 
 1099: \subsection{Target-Query Gated Multi-Head Attention}
 1100: \label{subsubsec:target_query_attention}
 1101: 
 1102: The principal Stage~2 model, \methodfield{target_query_gated}, does not update every historical token through pairwise self-attention. Instead, the current flow supplies a single query over its eligible history. Let $\mathbf{c}_i$ denote the projected Stage~1 representation of the current flow and $\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{K_i\times d}$ the age-encoded history. Attention head $h$ computes
 1103: 
 1104: \begin{align}
 1105:     \mathbf{q}^{h}_i
 1106:     &=W_Q^{h}\operatorname{LN}
 1107:       \left(\mathbf{c}_i+\operatorname{PE}(0)\right),\\
 1108:     \mathbf{K}^{h}_i
 1109:     &=\operatorname{LN}(\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_i)W_K^{h},\\
 1110:     \mathbf{V}^{h}_i
 1111:     &=\operatorname{LN}(\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_i)W_V^{h},\\
 1112:     \mathbf{h}^{h}_i
 1113:     &=\operatorname{softmax}
 1114:       \left(
 1115:         \frac{\mathbf{q}^{h}_i(\mathbf{K}^{h}_i)^{\top}}
 1116:              {\sqrt{d_h}}+M_i
 1117:       \right)
 1118:       \mathbf{V}^{h}_i.
 1119:     \label{eq:target_query_attention}
 1120: \end{align}
 1121: 
 1122: Concatenation and projection of the head outputs produce the historical summary $\mathbf{h}_i$. When the eligible history is empty, $\mathbf{h}_i$ is set explicitly to zero so that an artificial padding item cannot supply context.
 1123: 
 1124: Difference and multiplicative terms compare the current representation with its context summary:
 1125: 
 1126: \begin{align}
 1127:     \mathbf{r}_i
 1128:     &=[\mathbf{c}_i;\mathbf{h}_i;
 1129:        \mathbf{c}_i-\mathbf{h}_i;
 1130:        \mathbf{c}_i\odot\mathbf{h}_i],\\
 1131:     \mathbf{v}_i
 1132:     &=[\mathbf{h}_i;
 1133:        \mathbf{c}_i-\mathbf{h}_i;
 1134:        \mathbf{c}_i\odot\mathbf{h}_i].
 1135:     \label{eq:target_context_interactions}
 1136: \end{align}
 1137: 
 1138: A dimension-wise gate controls the subsequent context update:
 1139: 
 1140: \begin{align}
 1141:     \mathbf{g}_i&=\sigma(G(\mathbf{r}_i)),\\
 1142:     \mathbf{d}_i&=U(\mathbf{v}_i),\\
 1143:     \mathbf{f}_i&=\operatorname{LN}
 1144:       \left(\mathbf{c}_i+\mathbf{g}_i\odot\mathbf{d}_i\right).
 1145:     \label{eq:gated_context_update}
 1146: \end{align}
 1147: 
 1148: If no history exists, $\mathbf{g}_i$ and $\mathbf{d}_i$ are both zero and the current-flow representation passes through unchanged. A residual feed-forward block then produces
 1149: 
 1150: \begin{align}
 1151:     \mathbf{z}^{(2)}_i
 1152:     &=\operatorname{LN}
 1153:       \left(
 1154:         \mathbf{f}_i+\operatorname{FFN}(\mathbf{f}_i)
 1155:       \right),\\
 1156:     \boldsymbol{\ell}^{(2)}_i
 1157:     &=h_2(\mathbf{z}^{(2)}_i).
 1158:     \label{eq:stage2_output}
 1159: \end{align}
 1160: 
 1161: Target-query attention preserves a direct path for the current flow and uses history as a learned correction. Since there is only one query, the attention-score matrix grows linearly rather than forming the $W\times W$ matrix required by full self-attention. Multi-head retrieval over a 128-flow window is therefore computationally practical.
 1162: \section{Imbalance-Aware Objective and Stage Interface}
 1163: \label{subsec:method_training}
 1164: 
 1165: \subsection{Focal Objective and Controlled Sampling}
 1166: \label{subsubsec:focal_training}
 1167: 
 1168: Two-class softmax outputs and focal loss are used in both stages. For target $y_i$ with assigned-class probability $p_{i,y_i}$,
 1169: 
 1170: \begin{equation}
 1171:     \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{focal}}
 1172:     =-\frac{1}{B}
 1173:       \sum_{i=1}^{B}
 1174:       \alpha_{y_i}
 1175:       \left(1-p_{i,y_i}\right)^{\gamma}
 1176:       \log p_{i,y_i}.
 1177:     \label{eq:method_focal_loss}
 1178: \end{equation}
 1179: 
 1180: Originally introduced for severe foreground/background imbalance, focal loss downweights examples that are already classified easily \cite{Lin2017FocalLoss}. Table~\ref{tab:experimental_training_config} reports the chosen focal exponent, class weights, and label-smoothing values.
 1181: 
 1182: A controlled weighted sampler additionally determines training batches. Given class counts $n_0$ and $n_1$ and desired expected positive share $q$, negative and positive examples receive
 1183: 
 1184: \begin{equation}
 1185:     w_0=\frac{1-q}{n_0},
 1186:     \qquad
 1187:     w_1=\frac{q}{n_1}.
 1188:     \label{eq:controlled_sampler}
 1189: \end{equation}
 1190: 
 1191: Sampling changes expected minority exposure but leaves both the underlying dataset and the loss definition intact. Chapter~\ref{sec:experimental_methodology} specifies stage-specific target proportions and loader policies.
 1192: 
 1193: \subsection{Sequential Stage Interface}
 1194: \label{subsec:sequential_stage_interface}
 1195: 
 1196: After Stage~1 has been trained for flow classification, its validation-selected checkpoint is restored. A deterministic loader without sampling exports one $\mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i$ and the required context metadata for every flow. These embeddings remain fixed during Stage~2 training, and no Stage~2 gradient reaches the earlier encoder. The sequential interface consequently allows Stage~1 comparisons to vary intra-flow representation while all Stage~2 models reason over a common representation.
