 1206: \chapter{Experimental Methodology}
 1207: \label{sec:experimental_methodology}
 1208: 
 1209: Within a comparison block, the data manifest, fitted preprocessing state, optimisation budget, checkpoint criterion, threshold rule, and random seed remain fixed. Validation PR-AUC determines the checkpoint, and validation class-1 F1 determines the decision threshold. The untouched test partition is evaluated only after both choices have been fixed. This protocol limits temporal leakage and feedback from test results, two sources of optimistic NIDS estimates \cite{SommerPaxson2010,Arp2022DosDonts}.
 1210: 
 1211: \section{Evaluation Manifests and Leakage Controls}
 1212: \label{subsec:experimental_data_protocol}
 1213: 
 1214: Evaluation is organised around two chronological manifests. Comparisons of Stage~1 architectures, encodings, fusion schemes, sequence lengths, and selection policies use 35,410 test flows: 34,003 negative and 1,407 positive. The hierarchical Scheme~C reference and every Stage~2 experiment instead use the complete 84,380-flow test partition, comprising 80,860 negative and 3,520 positive flows. Results from these manifests are not paired, even when their rounded values happen to be close; effect sizes are calculated only between models sharing the same manifest.
 1215: 
 1216: All models within a block receive identical flows, packet and flow tensors, partition assignments, and train-fitted preprocessing. Training data alone determine category vocabularies, clipping limits, scaling parameters, and sampler weights. Configured class weights and sampling targets are also chosen without test feedback; neither validation nor test observations contribute to fitted transformations.
 1217: 
 1218: One frozen Stage~1 checkpoint supplies all Stage~2 inputs. Under online context construction, only embeddings earlier in stable start-time order are eligible. Validation targets may consult preceding training flows, while test targets may consult preceding flows from training, validation, or testing. A history is updated after the current context has been assembled, and it never exposes future embeddings or past labels. Embedding files, metadata ordering, context indices, and masks are reused across Stage~2 architectures, isolating the encoder or relation under study from context availability. Section~\ref{subsubsec:context_relations} distinguishes this start-ordered condition from strict availability at flow-completion time.
 1219: 
 1220: \subsection{External Evaluation Protocol}
 1221: \label{subsec:external_evaluation_protocol}
 1222: 
 1223: External evaluation keeps \datasetfield{ar002_et12_20260511_001} as the source training dataset and does not merge either external dataset into the source chronological partitions. The source-trained Stage~1 and Stage~2 checkpoints are applied to compatible external-test artefacts. External labels are reserved for metric calculation. Feature order, training-fitted clipping and scaling parameters, categorical vocabularies, model checkpoints, context relation, context capacity, and the source-validation decision threshold remain unchanged. No external test label is used for checkpoint selection, early stopping, threshold selection, or model comparison.
 1224: 
 1225: For CICIDS2017, the external flows are kept in chronological order and divided into non-overlapping 20,000-flow windows. Before model-performance comparison, windows are screened by class composition: label~1 must remain less frequent than label~0, matching the qualitative imbalance direction of the source dataset. This prevalence criterion yields \texttt{w01}, \texttt{w02}, \texttt{w06}, \texttt{w07}, and \texttt{w08}; it is not defined from their predictive scores. Stage~2 histories are constructed only from flows earlier than the target within the permitted external evaluation stream, and labels are never included in a history. Each reported window retains its original class composition. Results are presented separately by window and after pooling the five confusion matrices. Pooled ROC-AUC and PR-AUC require the flow-level scores to be concatenated and recomputed; they are therefore not inferred from confusion matrices or averaged and presented as a single-sample ranking measure.
 1226: 
 1227: The company-provided firewall capture is evaluated as one 38,513-flow cross-server set. Since it originates from a different server environment, it tests environmental transfer while remaining organisational data. External scores are not used to replace the internal chronological test: the internal test measures later source-domain traffic, whereas the two external evaluations measure public cross-dataset and company cross-server transfer.
