 1349: \section{Evaluation Metrics}
 1350: \label{subsec:experimental_metrics}
 1351: 
 1352: Metric calculations follow scikit-learn definitions \cite{Pedregosa2011ScikitLearn}. The alert-derived positive class is label~1 and the negative class label~0. Confusion matrices use the fixed orientation
 1353: \begin{equation}
 1354:     \mathbf{C}=
 1355:     \begin{bmatrix}
 1356:         \mathrm{TN} & \mathrm{FP}\\
 1357:         \mathrm{FN} & \mathrm{TP}
 1358:     \end{bmatrix},
 1359:     \label{eq:experimental_confusion_matrix}
 1360: \end{equation}
 1361: with true labels on rows and predictions on columns. Fixing this convention removes ambiguity when matrices are transferred between software packages.
 1362: 
 1363: \subsection{Class-Wise and Macro-Averaged Measures}
 1364: \label{subsubsec:macro_metrics}
 1365: 
 1366: For $c\in\{0,1\}$, class-wise precision, recall, and F1 are
 1367: \begin{align}
 1368:     P_c &= \frac{\mathrm{TP}_c}
 1369:     {\mathrm{TP}_c+\mathrm{FP}_c}, \\
 1370:     R_c &= \frac{\mathrm{TP}_c}
 1371:     {\mathrm{TP}_c+\mathrm{FN}_c}, \\
 1372:     F_{1,c} &= \frac{2P_cR_c}{P_c+R_c}.
 1373:     \label{eq:classwise_metrics}
 1374: \end{align}
 1375: Undefined ratios take the value zero, consistent with the implementation's
 1376: \texttt{zero\_division=0} setting. The macro-averaged metrics are
 1377: \begin{align}
 1378:     P_{\mathrm{macro}} &= \frac{P_0+P_1}{2}, \\
 1379:     R_{\mathrm{macro}} &= \frac{R_0+R_1}{2}, \\
 1380:     F_{1,\mathrm{macro}} &= \frac{F_{1,0}+F_{1,1}}{2}.
 1381:     \label{eq:experimental_macro_metrics}
 1382: \end{align}
 1383: 
 1384: Macro-F1 is the primary F1 measure and is abbreviated to ``F1'' in high-level tables. It assigns equal weight to the negative and positive classes despite their unequal prevalence; macro-precision and macro-recall are interpreted analogously. To reveal error changes hidden by macro averaging, $P_1$, $R_1$, and $F_{1,1}$ are reported alongside these summaries.
 1385: 
 1386: Accuracy and weighted averages remain secondary: an always-negative rule would score approximately $96.055\%$ accuracy on the raw data while detecting no positive flow. Neither high accuracy nor weighted F1 alone is therefore sufficient evidence of effective intrusion detection.
 1387: 
 1388: \subsection{False-Positive Rate and Operational Error Counts}
 1389: \label{subsubsec:fpr_metric}
 1390: 
 1391: False-positive rate is defined as
 1392: \begin{equation}
 1393:     \mathrm{FPR}=\frac{\mathrm{FP}}{\mathrm{FP}+\mathrm{TN}},
 1394:     \label{eq:false_positive_rate}
 1395: \end{equation}
 1396: and false-negative rate as
 1397: \begin{equation}
 1398:     \mathrm{FNR}=\frac{\mathrm{FN}}{\mathrm{FN}+\mathrm{TP}}
 1399:     =1-R_1.
 1400:     \label{eq:false_negative_rate}
 1401: \end{equation}
 1402: Even a low FPR can generate substantial alert volume in a high-throughput network. It is therefore considered together with absolute FP and FN counts. At the evaluated operating point, a test partition with $N_0$ negative flows produces an expected $N_0\times\mathrm{FPR}$ false alerts.
 1403: 
 1404: \subsection{Threshold-Independent Ranking Measures}
 1405: \label{subsubsec:ranking_metrics}
 1406: 
 1407: ROC-AUC measures ranking through the true-positive rate over the range of false-positive rates \cite{Fawcett2006ROC}. Under strong class imbalance, however, a large negative population can coexist with a high ROC-AUC. PR-AUC concentrates on retrieval of the positive class and consequently receives greater emphasis here \cite{SaitoRehmsmeier2015}.
 1408: 
 1409: The reported \texttt{pr\_auc} field is produced by scikit-learn's \texttt{average\_precision\_score}, rather than by trapezoidal integration under linearly interpolated precision--recall points:
 1410: \begin{equation}
 1411:     \mathrm{PR\mbox{-}AUC}=\sum_{k}(R_k-R_{k-1})P_k,
 1412:     \label{eq:average_precision}
 1413: \end{equation}
 1414: where $P_k$ and $R_k$ are evaluated at successive score thresholds. The name PR-AUC is used consistently throughout the thesis for this implementation. Since random ranking has an expected PR-AUC near positive prevalence, observed values should be interpreted against the class-1 share of the relevant test manifest.
 1415: 
 1416: \section{Efficiency and Resource Measurement}
 1417: \label{subsec:efficiency_protocol}
 1418: 
 1419: Experiments ran in Google Colaboratory Pro+ on an NVIDIA L4 GPU with 24~GB memory, using a company-funded allocation of 600 compute units \cite{GoogleColabFAQ2026,NVIDIAL4ProductBrief}. Those units regulate access to hosted resources and do not quantify a fixed amount of GPU computation. Runtime is compared only within the same stage because Stage~1 and Stage~2 differ in data loading, early stopping, and execution path.
 1420: 
 1421: Trainable parameters are counted directly from tensors that require gradients. Stage~1 wall time includes fitting, validation, and checkpointing. It is treated as a descriptive single-run cost because early stopping and variation in the hosted environment affect duration. For Stage~2 timing, gradients are disabled and the model is placed in evaluation mode. Per-flow latency is calculated as
 1422: \begin{equation}
 1423:     t_{\mathrm{flow}}=\frac{T_{\mathrm{test}}}{N_{\mathrm{test}}},
 1424:     \qquad
 1425:     Q=\frac{N_{\mathrm{test}}}{T_{\mathrm{test}}},
 1426:     \label{eq:inference_efficiency}
 1427: \end{equation}
 1428: where $Q$ denotes flows processed per second. Timing starts after Stage~1 embeddings and context indices have been prepared, so it measures model evaluation rather than the complete path from PCAP to alert.
 1429: 
 1430: \begin{table}[H]
 1431:     \centering
 1432:     \caption{Execution environment for the reported experiment blocks.}
 1433:     \label{tab:experimental_environment}
 1434:     \begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.4cm}p{10.0cm}@{}}
 1435:         \toprule
 1436:         Component & Recorded environment \\
 1437:         \midrule
 1438:         Hosted platform & \ExpPlatform \\
 1439:         Purchased allocation & \ExpComputeBudget \\
 1440:         Accelerator & \ExpGPU \\
 1441:         Host processor and RAM & \ExpCPU \\
 1442:         Operating environment & \ExpOS \\
 1443:         Learning software & \ExpSoftware \\
 1444:         Traffic extractor & \ExpSuricata \\
 1445:         \bottomrule
 1446:     \end{tabular}
 1447: \end{table}
 1448: 
 1449: Available run metadata do not retain the exact CPU, software snapshot, Suricata version, ruleset snapshot, configuration, custom-module revision, or PCAP checksum. Exact reproduction of runtime and label generation is consequently limited, and the missing provenance is identified as unavailable rather than reconstructed after the fact.
