\chapter{Results and Discussion}
\label{sec:results}

Unless explicitly qualified, F1, precision, and recall refer to macro averages; class-1 quantities carry a separate label. Discussion centres on macro-F1, PR-AUC, class-1 performance, FP, FN, and FPR because the majority class dominates accuracy. Numerical differences are interpreted only when the models share a test manifest.

\section{Stage~1: Intra-Flow Representation}
\label{subsec:results_stage1}

\subsection{Architecture and Temporal-Encoding Effects}
\label{subsubsec:results_stage1_models}

Stage~1 model families and encoding ablations are contrasted on a common flow test set in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_models}; their parameter counts and training times appear in Table~\ref{tab:results_stage1_errors_efficiency}.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\resultsfigurepath/stage1_model_comparison.pdf}
    \caption{Stage~1 architecture and encoding comparison on the common flow test manifest. The horizontal axis begins at 0.52, and absolute values are printed at the end of each bar.}
    \label{fig:results_stage1_models}
\end{figure}

Among these models, position+time gives the Transformer the best overall balance: macro-F1 reaches $0.8882$ and PR-AUC $0.8564$. LSTM+time is the strongest non-Transformer alternative by macro-F1, yet the Transformer improves this score by 2.05 percentage points. At the selected operating point, 95 additional positive flows are recovered at the cost of 10 more false positives. The gain therefore reflects a materially different error balance rather than a change in a summary score alone.

Encoding ablations separate two meanings of sequence location. Position records where a packet occurs, whereas cumulative time distinguishes a rapid burst from an identical order spread over a longer interval. Time-only encoding exceeds position-only, and combining them adds another 0.64 percentage points of macro-F1. Both FP and FN are also lower than under no encoding. These changes support order and elapsed time as complementary, rather than interchangeable, descriptions of a flow.

The lowest FPR, $0.8293\%$, belongs to the GRU, but that operating point misses 437 positive flows. Position+time introduces only 14 additional FP and removes 131 FN relative to the GRU. A low false-alert rate is therefore not sufficient by itself: conservative thresholds can suppress FP by transferring error to the positive class.

The flow-statistics MLP provides the direct aggregation-only control. It obtains macro-F1 $0.7762$ and PR-AUC $0.5604$ on the same test composition, compared with $0.8882$ and $0.8564$ for position+time. Its 853 FP and 501 FN fall to 296 and 306 when packet dynamics are modelled. Duration, count, and inter-arrival summaries therefore retain useful global information but fail to reconstruct all of the local order and timing present in the packet sequence.

Operating-point errors are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_errors}. Because the negative examples are shared, FP bar height is directly proportional to FPR.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\resultsfigurepath/stage1_error_comparison.pdf}
    \caption{Stage~1 false-positive and false-negative counts on the common flow test manifest. FPR is printed beside each FP count.}
    \label{fig:results_stage1_errors}
\end{figure}

LSTM also benefits from explicit time, with reductions in both error types. For CNN, however, the effect is mixed: the selected operating point improves, while PR-AUC declines and FN rises. This disagreement is informative. Thresholded F1 characterises one decision point, whereas PR-AUC evaluates ranking over thresholds. Timing is therefore not beneficial merely because it is present; its representation within the encoder matters, and an appended interval feature is not equivalent to an elapsed-time sequence coordinate.

\begin{table}[H]
    \centering
    \caption{Stage~1 parameter counts and single-run training times measured on an NVIDIA L4 GPU in Google Colab.}
    \label{tab:results_stage1_errors_efficiency}
    \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
        \toprule
        Model & Trainable parameters & Training time (s) & Training time (min) \\
        \midrule
        Position+time Transformer & 344,963 & 4,337.6 & 72.29 \\
        Time-only Transformer & 344,963 & 2,250.5 & 37.51 \\
        Position-only Transformer & 344,963 & 1,994.7 & 33.25 \\
        LSTM+time & 217,346 & 3,556.3 & 59.27 \\
        CNN1D+time & 70,274 & 1,162.4 & 19.37 \\
        GRU & 162,690 & 3,684.4 & 61.41 \\
        LSTM & 216,834 & 3,381.2 & 56.35 \\
        CNN1D & 68,354 & 1,591.0 & 26.52 \\
        BiLSTM & 564,738 & 2,625.7 & 43.76 \\
        No-encoding Transformer & 344,963 & 2,213.9 & 36.90 \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}

No MLP efficiency entry is shown in Table~\ref{tab:results_stage1_errors_efficiency}, as its parameter count and training time were not retained.

CNN1D+time records the shortest Stage~1 training time, 1,162.4 seconds; position+time requires 4,337.6 seconds. Despite the BiLSTM's larger parameter count, the proposed encoder is the most expensive run observed. Each value was collected on an NVIDIA L4 GPU, but it includes early stopping, data loading, and variation in the hosted runtime. These measurements describe total training cost, not architecture-only throughput.

\subsection{Packet and Flow-Statistics Integration}
\label{subsubsec:results_stage1_fusion}

The fusion comparison in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_fusion} uses one shared flow manifest. Scheme~A tiles the statistical vector across packet tokens, Scheme~B retains packet inputs alone, and Scheme~C conditions packet representations through bounded token-FiLM from a separate branch.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\resultsfigurepath/stage1_fusion_comparison.pdf}
    \caption{Controlled Stage~1 integration comparison on the common flow manifest. Left: macro metrics and threshold-independent ranking metrics. Right: false-positive and false-negative counts. The score axis is truncated and explicitly labelled.}
    \label{fig:results_stage1_fusion}
\end{figure}

Removing the tiled vector in Scheme~B raises macro-F1 by 1.30 percentage points and PR-AUC by 2.38 points over Scheme~A, although Scheme~A retains a small macro-precision advantage. Repetition of one aggregate vector at every position may therefore interfere with distinctions among packet tokens. Scheme~C strengthens this interpretation: flow statistics appear more useful when encoded through a dedicated pathway than when copied into each packet position.

Scheme~C is strongest overall, with macro-F1 $0.8954$, PR-AUC $0.8763$, 267 FP, and 293 FN. Both error counts improve over packet-only Scheme~B, indicating that aggregate statistics add information when they condition rather than replace packet evidence. Attribution must nevertheless remain at the level of the integrated design. Scheme~C introduces both extra parameters and a separate statistical pathway, so the present ablation cannot assign the entire gain specifically to token-FiLM. A parameter-matched concatenation control would be needed for that narrower claim.

\subsection{Sequence Length and Packet Selection}
\label{subsubsec:results_sequence_length}

Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_sensitivity} presents both sensitivity analyses: sequence length is traced in the line panel, and selection policies are compared through grouped precision and recall bars.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
    {\resultsfigurepath/stage1_sensitivity.pdf}
    \caption{Stage~1 sensitivity analysis. Left: maximum packet-sequence length under head selection. Right: packet-selection strategy at $L=128$. Dashed lines identify the selected sequence length. Truncated axes are explicitly labelled.}
    \label{fig:results_stage1_sensitivity}
\end{figure}

Longer sequences do not produce monotonic improvement. Ranking is weaker when only a few early packets are available, implying that those packets are not always sufficient for discrimination. Performance peaks at $L=128$ by macro-F1 and overall error balance. Increasing the budget to 256 adds 46 FP and lowers both macro-F1 and PR-AUC; additional packets can therefore add volume without adding useful evidence.

Aggregate sweep results do not identify the mechanism behind this peak. Changing $L$ affects long truncated flows but leaves sufficiently short flows unchanged, so dilution by later packets cannot be separated from flow-length composition or optimisation effects. Such an explanation would require length-stratified evaluation. Consequently, $L=128$ is treated as the best observed budget for this capture, not a universal horizon.

At $L=128$, the macro-F1 difference between head and head-tail is only 0.16 percentage points. With one seed, this margin is treated as practically inconclusive. Random selection shifts the detector towards lower FPR and more FN. Head remains the principal policy because it is deterministic, provides the best observed macro-F1 and attack-class recall, and preserves a contiguous early prefix without waiting for the flow's final packets. Its small advantage over head-tail is not interpreted as a robust ordering.

\section{Stage~2: Inter-Flow Context}
\label{subsec:results_stage2}

\subsection{Gain over Independent Flow Classification}
\label{subsubsec:results_hierarchical_gain}

The principal cross-stage comparison appears in Table~\ref{tab:results_hierarchical_gain}. Both rows share the same test flows: one classifies each Scheme~C embedding independently, and the other augments that frozen representation with causal source-host history.

\begin{table}[H]
    \centering
    \caption{Independent Stage~1 classification and the complete hierarchical system on the common full-data manifest.}
    \label{tab:results_hierarchical_gain}
    \resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
    \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrr@{}}
        \toprule
        Model & $P_{\mathrm{macro}}$ & $R_{\mathrm{macro}}$ &
        $F_{1,\mathrm{macro}}$ & $F_{1,1}$ & ROC-AUC & PR-AUC & FPR (\%) \\
        \midrule
        Stage~1 Scheme C & 0.8990 & 0.8919 & 0.8954 & 0.7994 & 0.9890 &
        0.8763 & 0.8249 \\
        Stage~2 source-host & \textbf{0.9328} & \textbf{0.9224} &
        \textbf{0.9275} & \textbf{0.8610} & \textbf{0.9938} &
        \textbf{0.9345} & \textbf{0.5429} \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}%
    }
\end{table}

Adding Stage~2 raises macro-F1 by 3.21 percentage points and PR-AUC by 5.82 points relative to independent Scheme~C classification. The contextual system removes 228 FP and 205 FN, a relative FPR reduction of approximately 34.2\%. Since both error types decline, the gain is not an accuracy artefact or a simple shift towards one class. This comparison concerns the hierarchy as an integrated system. Subsequent architecture controls test simpler encoders, but the capacity mismatch of the no-context head prevents the full difference from being attributed solely to historical evidence.


\subsection{Which Relation Defines Useful Context?}
\label{subsubsec:results_context_relation}

Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage2_context} links ranking quality to operating behaviour. Macro-F1 and PR-AUC are shown on the left; class-1 recall and FPR locate each relation on the right.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\resultsfigurepath/stage2_context_analysis.pdf}
    \caption{Stage~2 context-relation ablation at $W=128$. Left: macro-F1 and PR-AUC. Right: class-1 recall versus FPR, where the desirable region is the upper-left corner.}
    \label{fig:results_stage2_context}
\end{figure}

Source-host history yields the strongest balance: macro-F1 is $0.9275$, PR-AUC $0.9345$, and FPR is the lowest among the relations. Repeated outbound activity from one initiator may preserve evidence of scanning, retries, or coordinated connections. Global recency, by comparison, mixes unrelated flows, while destination history may combine many benign clients of a widely used service. This interpretation agrees with relational and window-level findings in prior NIDS work \cite{Deng2023FlowTopology,MartinReizabal2026FlowWindow}, but it remains a mechanism hypothesis because binary labels do not identify the attack families responsible for the gain.

Endpoint context operates at a different point: fewer positives are missed, but more false alerts are produced. Matching either endpoint in either role can retrieve lateral or bidirectional activity, while also admitting a more heterogeneous neighbourhood. It may be appropriate when FN carry a higher operational cost; under the balanced criterion used here, source-host remains preferable. Time-only context ranks flows less effectively and produces more FN, suggesting that chronological proximity without an entity relation is insufficiently selective for this capture.

\subsection{How Much Context Is Useful?}
\label{subsubsec:results_context_window}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
    {\resultsfigurepath/stage2_window_sensitivity.pdf}
    \caption{Source-host context-window sensitivity. Left: macro-F1 and PR-AUC. Right: false-positive and false-negative counts. The dashed line marks the selected window $W=128$.}
    \label{fig:results_stage2_window}
\end{figure}

Context capacity is also non-monotonic in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage2_window}. A 32-flow window maximises macro-recall, but its 775 FP show the cost of that sensitivity. At 128 flows, macro-F1 reaches $0.9275$, PR-AUC reaches $0.9345$, and FPR falls to its minimum of $0.5429\%$, giving the strongest joint outcome. Both FP and FN rise when the window expands to 256. This pattern is compatible with a finite useful horizon: short histories may omit relevant activity, while increasingly old flows from the same source may have little connection to the current event.

Nominal window size alone cannot explain this response. The run summaries do not retain target-level counts or age distributions for valid context items, so older evidence, padding frequency, and unequal history coverage cannot be disentangled. Performance stratified by realised context length and elapsed span would be needed to test the horizon explanation directly. Within the present design, $W=128$ is therefore an empirical operating point for this capture, not a universal optimum.

\subsection{Stage~2 Architecture Comparison}
\label{subsubsec:results_stage2_architecture_comparison}

Architecture comparison fixes the Stage~1 embeddings, source-host relation, $W=128$, online policy, objective, optimiser, sampler, checkpoint criterion, and validation threshold search. Only the context encoder and dimensions specific to that encoder vary. Six architectures are displayed in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage2_architectures}; FP and FN are shown separately in the second panel so that aggregate F1 does not conceal their trade-off.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
    {\resultsfigurepath/stage2_architecture_comparison.pdf}
    \caption{Stage~2 architecture comparison on the common flow test manifest. Left: macro-F1, class-1 F1, and PR-AUC. Right: threshold-dependent false-positive and false-negative counts. Truncated score axes are labelled.}
    \label{fig:results_stage2_architectures}
\end{figure}

Target-query attention produces the best combined result, with macro-F1 $0.9275$, class-1 F1 $0.8610$, and PR-AUC $0.9345$. The nearest baseline values are CNN+LSTM's $0.9097$ macro-F1 and GRU's $0.9141$ PR-AUC. LSTM has slightly higher macro-recall, yet that advantage is accompanied by weaker threshold-independent ranking and a less favourable false-alert balance.

Confusion matrices make these differences operationally concrete. Against the vanilla Transformer, target-query attention eliminates 177 FP and 117 FN on the same flows. LSTM recovers 28 more positives than the proposed model but generates 338 additional false alerts. The macro-F1 gain over vanilla attention is therefore not obtained by exchanging one error type for the other, and the precision advantage over the highest-recall recurrent baseline is substantial.

This contrast is consistent with the intended inductive bias. Vanilla self-attention updates the full context sequence and classifies its final valid state; the proposed encoder keeps the current flow as the query and gates the retrieved historical summary. Simultaneous improvement in macro-F1 and PR-AUC is compatible with stronger target-centred ranking of positive examples. It is not, however, a clean isolation of the query pattern, because other architectural choices and parameter counts also differ.

With 514 parameters, the no-context head is far smaller than the 283,522-parameter Stage~2 model. It should be read as a current-flow-only lower bound, not a capacity-matched ablation. Its result shows that the minimal independent head is insufficient, but does not identify how much of the full gap comes from history. A stricter control would preserve the target-query architecture and comparable capacity while masking every historical item.

\subsection{Efficiency and Operational Trade-Offs}
\label{subsec:results_statistical_efficiency}

Parameter count and measured evaluation latency are related in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage2_efficiency}, with macro-F1 annotated for each architecture. A logarithmic parameter axis accommodates the range from 514 to more than 1.2 million parameters.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]
    {\resultsfigurepath/stage2_efficiency.pdf}
    \caption{Stage~2 performance--efficiency trade-off. Latency covers model evaluation after Stage~1 embeddings and context indices have been built; it is not end-to-end PCAP-to-alert latency.}
    \label{fig:results_stage2_efficiency}
\end{figure}

All profiles in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage2_efficiency} were collected on an NVIDIA L4. The proposed model has 283,522 parameters and evaluates one flow in 0.1965~ms on average, equivalent to approximately 5,089 flows per second for the measured model path. Vanilla Transformer latency is 0.1967~ms. A difference of 0.0002~ms is too small to support a speed claim; the meaningful observation is latency parity accompanied by a better error balance.

Compared with GRU, LSTM, and CNN+LSTM, parameter count falls by $59.0\%$, $69.3\%$, and $76.7\%$, respectively; measured latency falls by $42.2\%$, $43.3\%$, and $45.2\%$. Unlike the negligible margin over vanilla attention, these differences indicate a genuine compactness and model-level latency advantage over recurrent alternatives. They are not estimates of deployed end-to-end throughput. Context indexing, Stage~1 inference, Suricata extraction, and PCAP processing are excluded, and one aggregate timing cannot characterise warm-up, run-to-run variation, batching effects, or tail latency.

\section{External Generalisation}
\label{subsec:results_external_generalisation}

The source models are trained on \datasetfield{ar002_et12_20260511_001}. External evaluation then considers the public CICIDS2017 Wednesday-workingHours capture and the company-provided \datasetfield{firewall_test_20250731_160700} capture from a different server. Table~\ref{tab:results_external_scores} reports each chronological CICIDS2017 window separately so that temporal heterogeneity is not hidden by one aggregate score.

\begin{table}[H]
    \centering
    \caption{External evaluation on prevalence-compatible public CICIDS2017 Wednesday windows and a separate company server capture.}
    \label{tab:results_external_scores}
    \resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
    \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrr@{}}
        \toprule
        Dataset/window & Flows & Positive (\%) & Accuracy &
        $F_{1,\mathrm{macro}}$ & $F_{1,1}$ & ROC-AUC & PR-AUC \\
        \midrule
        CICIDS2017 w01 & 20,000 & 18.195 & 0.9478 & 0.9125 & 0.8569 & 0.9816 & 0.9088 \\
        CICIDS2017 w02 & 20,000 & 13.515 & 0.9636 & 0.9226 & 0.8663 & 0.9728 & 0.9123 \\
        CICIDS2017 w06 & 20,000 & 16.480 & 0.9300 & 0.8536 & 0.7479 & 0.9758 & 0.8906 \\
        CICIDS2017 w07 & 20,000 & 13.340 & 0.9358 & 0.8640 & 0.7651 & 0.9691 & 0.8419 \\
        CICIDS2017 w08 & 20,000 & 15.235 & 0.9583 & 0.9220 & 0.8688 & 0.9884 & 0.9397 \\
        Company cross-server & 38,513 & 3.285 & 0.9882 & 0.9026 & 0.8112 & 0.9870 & 0.8771 \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}%
    }
\end{table}

Across the five public windows selected by the minority-positive prevalence criterion, the arithmetic mean of window-level macro-F1 is 0.8949, with a range from 0.8536 to 0.9226. Pooling their confusion matrices gives 82,292 TN, 2,355 FP, 2,937 FN, and 12,416 TP over 100,000 flows. The resulting pooled macro-F1 is 0.8966, class-1 F1 is 0.8243, and FPR is 2.7821\%. This pooled macro-F1 is not identical to the mean window-level score because the former is recomputed after aggregating counts. A pooled PR-AUC or ROC-AUC is not claimed here because those ranking measures require concatenated flow-level probabilities rather than confusion-matrix counts.

\begin{table}[H]
    \centering
    \caption{External-test confusion matrices and operating-point error rates.}
    \label{tab:results_external_errors}
    \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
        \toprule
        Dataset/window & TN & FP & FN & TP & FPR (\%) \\
        \midrule
        CICIDS2017 w01 & 15,830 & 531 & 513 & 3,126 & 3.2468 \\
        CICIDS2017 w02 & 16,914 & 383 & 345 & 2,358 & 2.2143 \\
        CICIDS2017 w06 & 16,521 & 183 & 1,218 & 2,078 & 1.0955 \\
        CICIDS2017 w07 & 16,622 & 710 & 575 & 2,093 & 4.0965 \\
        CICIDS2017 w08 & 16,405 & 548 & 286 & 2,761 & 3.2325 \\
        \midrule
        CICIDS2017 pooled & 82,292 & 2,355 & 2,937 & 12,416 & 2.7821 \\
        Company cross-server & 37,087 & 161 & 292 & 973 & 0.4322 \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The CICIDS2017 results demonstrate meaningful but non-uniform public cross-dataset transfer within windows where label~1 remains the minority. Windows w01 and w02 provide balanced class-1 precision and recall, while w08 gives the strongest class-1 F1 and PR-AUC. Window w06 behaves conservatively: its class-1 precision is 0.9191, but recall falls to 0.6305 and 1,218 positive flows are missed. Since ROC-AUC and PR-AUC remain 0.9758 and 0.8906, respectively, this pattern is compatible with probability or threshold miscalibration under temporal shift rather than complete loss of ranking ability. Window w07 exhibits a different failure mode. It has the largest FP count and the lowest PR-AUC, indicating weaker class separation in addition to its operating-point error balance.

On the separate company server, macro-F1 reaches 0.9026, class-1 F1 reaches 0.8112, and PR-AUC reaches 0.8771. The FPR remains low at 0.4322\%, but class-1 recall is 0.7692, corresponding to 292 FN among 1,265 positive flows. Cross-server degradation is therefore expressed primarily through missed positives rather than an increase in false alerts. Together, the public and organisational external tests show that the learned representation and source-host context retain useful signal outside the source capture, while the window-level variation cautions against treating external performance as invariant across time or environment.

These external comparisons do not isolate dataset shift to one factor. CICIDS2017 and the two company captures can differ in collection conditions, class prevalence, attack composition, host roles, flow construction, and label provenance. In addition, the source-host relation can change meaning under network address translation, shared services, or different host populations. The evidence therefore supports preliminary external generalisation under the implemented evaluation pipeline, not universal transfer to arbitrary networks.

\section{Cross-Stage Discussion}
\label{subsec:results_cross_stage_discussion}

Different losses of information are addressed at the two levels. Stage~1 restores order and elapsed time that aggregate vectors discard; Stage~2 introduces behavioural evidence absent from independent classification. Ablation results support both roles: position and time contribute complementary intra-flow information, and start-ordered source-host history improves the detector after its Stage~1 representation is fixed. In this respect, the hierarchy extends packet-aware systems such as GTID and modular flow encoders such as FlowTransformer with an explicitly evaluated cross-flow reasoning layer \cite{Han2023GTID,Manocchio2024FlowTransformer}.

With respect to RQ1, time-aware packet modelling exceeds the statistical MLP on a shared manifest, while fusion results show that aggregate measurements still help when used for conditioning rather than replacement. The sequence-length sweep qualifies this result: useful representation depends on which packets are retained and encoded, not on unlimited token growth. For RQ2, Stage~2 lowers both FP and FN relative to independent Scheme~C predictions. Relation and window experiments add a second qualification: context must be selective, and additional neighbouring flows are not automatically useful.

These findings accord with temporal sequence models and graph-based analyses that treat traffic records as dependent \cite{LiuPatras2022NetSentry,Deng2023FlowTopology,Xu2024SSLGNN}. External evaluation further shows that useful signal persists across a public benchmark and a different company server, although its strength and error balance vary by environment and chronological window. The results do not establish source-host history as the best relation for every attack, nor can binary labels reveal which families benefit. A stronger cross-stage analysis would align individual errors, identify which Stage~1 FP and FN are corrected by Stage~2, and relate those transitions to relation type, realised context size, and context age.

Taken together, the evidence indicates that temporal structure within a flow and selective relations across flows remedy different deficiencies of aggregate, independent flow classification.

\chapter{Conclusion and Future Work}

